Z={
1:"Vivekananda called fearlessness, abhīḥ, the central message of the Upaniṣads, and told young Indians that weakness was the root of every sin. Teachers describe the fearless person as one who has jumped into the river of the Self and found that it does not drown. Commentators note that dāna, giving, also appears early in the list: the divine nature is open-handed.",
2:"Commentators note dayā bhūteṣu, “compassion for beings”, and aloluptva, “freedom from greed”, side by side: compassion grows as greed shrinks. The Jain tradition, with its strict non-violence, sees these as one virtue. Many Hindu households begin the day by feeding birds or cows, a small daily practice of compassion for beings.",
3:"Commentators note that the word abhijāta, “born to”, suggests these qualities are a natural inheritance for some, the fruit of past lives. But Madhusūdana adds that they can also be cultivated. Benjamin Franklin famously kept a chart of thirteen virtues and worked on one each week; the list here could be used the same way.",
4:"Commentators note pāruṣya, harshness of speech, as a demonic quality: words can wound like weapons. The Mahābhārata gives Duryodhana’s insults to Draupadī in the dice hall as a terrible example, and they became one of the causes of the war. Darpa, arrogance, was the downfall of Rāvaṇa in the Rāmāyaṇa.",
5:"Commentators note that Kṛṣṇa calls Arjuna Pāṇḍava here, son of Pāṇḍu, a reminder of his noble lineage. Madhusūdana says Arjuna’s worry itself proves his divine nature, since the demonic do not worry about being demonic. Many readers find this verse comforting in times of self-doubt.",
6:"Commentators note that Kṛṣṇa speaks of two kinds of creation, bhūta-sargau. Śaṅkara says the same person can show both natures at different times. The Chāndogya Upaniṣad (1.2) tells how gods and demons fought, and the demons spoiled every sense with evil, but could not touch the vital breath. The deepest life in us remains pure.",
7:"Commentators note śauca, purity, and ācāra, right conduct, as basic marks missing in the demonic nature. Indian tradition gives great weight to ācāra, daily conduct, often more than to belief. The Manusmṛti calls right conduct the highest dharma. Without it, says the Gītā, truth too disappears.",
8:"Commentators note that the Gītā does not attack all materialist thought, but the conclusion that desire alone matters. Many later Indian thinkers engaged seriously with Cārvāka arguments, and some modern scholars see them as an early form of scepticism. The Gītā’s concern is practical: a world without meaning tends to become a world without restraint.",
9:"Commentators note naṣṭātmānaḥ, “lost souls”: the demonic have lost touch with their own Self. Madhusūdana says this is the deepest loss, deeper than any outer harm. History shows how ideas that deny all values can lead to great cruelty. The Gītā warns that the world itself suffers when such views gain power.",
10:"Commentators note mohād gṛhītvā, “seizing through delusion”: wrong views are grasped, not reasoned. Śaṅkara says delusion makes them hold false ideas firmly. Modern psychology speaks of confirmation bias, the tendency to hold on to what we already believe. The Gītā’s remedy is sattva: clear seeing, humility and good company.",
11:"Commentators note pralayāntām, “until death”: the worry lasts as long as life. The Bhāgavata tells of the courtesan Piṅgalā, who waited all night for clients and finally realised that hope brought only sorrow. She let go and slept peacefully. Her story (Bhāgavata 11.8) is told to show the peace of releasing endless wanting.",
12:"Commentators note kāma-krodha-parāyaṇāḥ, “given over to desire and anger”. Śaṅkara says those who gather wealth unjustly are driven by both. The Mahābhārata praises wealth earned by dharma and used for family, guests and the needy. Gandhi’s idea of trusteeship, holding wealth for others, draws on this ideal.",
13:"Commentators note the word manoratha, “wish” or “chariot of the mind”: the mind races ahead to the next desire. Śaṅkara says the demonic plan endlessly for gain. Many readers recognise this inner voice in themselves. Watching it with humour is one way to loosen its hold.",
14:"Commentators note siddho’ham, “I am successful”, and sukhī, “I am happy”: the ego claims happiness, yet the next verses show it is restless. Rāvaṇa in the Rāmāyaṇa spoke this way, proud of his power and wealth, until his fall. The epics often show pride followed by ruin.",
15:"Commentators note āḍhya, “wealthy”, and abhijanavān, “high-born”: wealth and birth are the two great sources of pride. Śaṅkara says the demonic think no one is equal to them. Many saints from humble backgrounds, such as Kabīr and Ravidās, challenged such pride in their poems.",
16:"Commentators note aneka-citta-vibhrānta, “bewildered by many thoughts”: the demonic mind is scattered. Śaṅkara says this scattering itself is a kind of hell. Meditation teachers often say that a scattered mind suffers more than a focused one, whatever the outer circumstances.",
17:"Commentators note stabdha, “stubborn” or “stiff”: the proud cannot bend. Śaṅkara says they are self-conceited and do not bow to the wise. Indian tradition values bowing, namaskāra, as a gesture of humility. The proud person who cannot bow cannot learn.",
18:"Commentators note bala, force, among the demonic traits: power used to dominate rather than protect. Śaṅkara says the demonic rely on force to get what they want. The epics distinguish the kṣatriya’s strength, used to protect, from the demon’s force, used to oppress. The same power can serve either.",
19:"Commentators note that this verse is among the hardest in the Gītā. Madhusūdana says the Lord is not angry, but the law of karma he upholds gives the cruel what their cruelty earns. The Buddhist Dhammapada begins with a similar law: speak or act with a corrupt mind, and suffering follows as the wheel follows the ox.",
20:"Commentators note mūḍhāḥ janmani janmani, “deluded, birth after birth”. Śaṅkara says the downward path continues while the demonic nature remains. The story of Ajāmila in the Bhāgavata shows that even late in life, a single sincere call can turn the path. The verse warns, but the Gītā as a whole leaves hope.",
21:"Commentators note the phrase nāśanam ātmanaḥ, “destructive of the self”: desire, anger and greed destroy the very person who feels them. Tulsīdās wrote that desire, anger, pride and greed are the paths to hell. Jain teaching names four passions, kaṣāyas: anger, pride, deceit and greed. Many traditions name similar gates.",
22:"Commentators note tamo-dvāra, “gates of darkness”: the three are named as doors into ignorance. Madhusūdana says closing them lets light in naturally. Practical teachers suggest watching for one gate at a time: a week of noticing anger, a week of noticing greed.",
23:"Commentators note that the verse names three losses: perfection, happiness and the supreme goal. Śaṅkara says those who ignore guidance lose all three. Many modern readers widen śāstra to include the wisdom of good teachers, elders and conscience.",
24:"Commentators note kāryākārya-vyavasthitau, “in deciding what to do and not do”: scripture is a guide for action, not only belief. Madhusūdana says the guidance of scripture is especially needed where the senses and reason cannot reach. The chapter ends by setting up Arjuna’s question in 17.1.",
}
